*8: Detailed comments for the authors
  In the context of distributed scientific workflows, this paper proposes a
memory-allocation framework that incorporates run-time memory usage data
(read bytes and mmap page faults), not considered by predictors in prior
work, into the memory prediction model. To keep eBPF profiling overhead
under control, an active learning selection policy is used to select
processes to be profiled while staying within a set profiling budget. The
experimental evaluation explores eBPF overhead, evaluates the CAMP allocator
against a peak-history baseline, and shows that CAMP reduces the number of
underallocations compared to the previously published method Sizey, though
at the cost of using more reserved capacity.


Paper strengths:
- Innovative idea.
- The problem is clearly formulated in Section 2.
- Comparison to prior work (Sizey); albeit a bit limited.


Paper weaknesses:
- The paper falls short in the discussion of the actual implementation of
 the proposed methodology and experimental evaluation.

- The description of the experimental evaluation is often unclear.

- The final experiment presented in Section 5-E is weak, not representative
 of diverse scientific workflows, as well as appears to contradict earlier
 findings in the paper.

- The "scope-based hierarchical architecture" is presented as one of the
 main contributions. However, in the paper it never becomes clear how
 these scopes are defined. What are common "compatibility criteria" that
 are used for these scopes? These scopes are also not considered nor
 explored in the experimental evaluation.


The paper presents an interesting and potentially innovative idea and
starts out well with a clear description of the problem in Section 2.
Unfortunately, as I get along in the paper in Section 3, and Section 4 in
particular, I became less convinced of the paper's methods. The paper falls
short in discussion of the actual implementation and in the experimental
evaluation, as I set out below. The final experiment is weak and because of
its specificity it does not convince that this method is worthwhile for
wider application in (bioinformatics) workflows.


Other remarks:

- It never fully became clear to me what an "multi-gate active learning
 selection policy" is. Section III-G is said to describe "the gates that
 determine the auditing policy". However, the word "gate" never occurs in
 this subsection.

- The paper focuses on theory, but lacks more technical details on
 implementation, e.g., how is the eBPF code implemented? How is eBPF
 incorporated into SLURM files? Do the existing workflows need adaptations
 to be used with CAMP? What is the role of nextflow? Is it required that a
 workflow uses nextflow? If so, is nextflow adapted to work with CAMP?
 Nextflow is named in Section IV-A, but its role and importance are never
 discussed.

- Section IV-A: what is "the matched offline cohort"? Is this data collected
 prior to this research? How was it collected? Was the AWS ParallelCluster
 involved in this case?

- Section IV-A: consider that this description of the various workloads is
 inaccessible to a more general BigData audience.

- Section IV-C: this section is unclear to me. So, a model is trained, but
 as part of this "development predictions" are produced by "models that
 were not trained"? What untrained models were used? How does this now all
 fit together?

- Section IV-C: "these controls support four research questions": please
 note that *five* research questions are listed. Also, the sentence ends
 with a closing quote instead of a colon.

- Section IV-D: Cov and MdAPE are not briefly described in the main text.

- Section V-A: what is a "consumed-data signal" ?

- Section V-A: what is the motivation for comparing to strace? strace cannot
 capture the same data? Why is this a good and representative comparison
 target for overhead?

- Section V-B: erroneously refers to Figures 2a(a), 2b(b) and 2b(a).

- Section V-B: where do the numbers for unused memory-time and total
 requested memory follow from? These are not shown in the referenced
 figure?

- Section V-C: what is "first-attempt coverage"? The number of tasks that
 succeed without OOM due to underallocation?

- Section V-C: given that Sizey has a large number of underallocated tasks,
 does that mean that Sizey has a high task failure rate? Is this common
 for and to be expected from Sizey?

- Section V-C: the authors seem to take for granted that CAMP uses
 significantly more reserved capacity than Sizey, even though in the
 Introduction it is pointed out that overestimation and conservative upper
 bounds should be avoided because these result in cluster inefficiency.
 Now, the question here is whether CAMP's higher reservations are really
 necessary. Are these conservative upper bounds or strict upper bounds to
 ensure that tasks will not fail due to OOM errors?

- Figure 3: the graph titles do not match the caption which is a little
 confusing. Moreover, the fact that for subgraph (c) the x-axis is
 different but with the same colors for the bars is more confusing. I also
 don't fully understand why the x-axis and the color bar legend have the
 same labels? Shouldn't the blue bar convey the Sizey baseline?

- Section V-E: what is a "logical Bowtie2 task" in contrast to a default
 Bowtie2 task?

- Section V-E: does each of the 6000 Bowtie2 tasks operate on the same data
 set, or on different data sets of different sizes?

- Section V-E: am I correct in assuming that the Bowtie2 tasks are
 independent of each other? So, isn't this experiment more representative
 of a SLURM job array rather than a scientific workflow that commonly
 consists of different components?

- Section V-E: how does Sizey perform on this benchmark? Why are no results
 reported for Sizey?

- Section V-E: am I right in understanding that a single "cold run" was done,
 followed by a single "warm run"? Wouldn't it for a simple scenario like
 this be the case that simple manual memory profiling can also lead to
 significantly improved memory estimation settings? In other words, how
 does this experiment convey that CAMP is suited for more complicated
 workflows that are run repeatedly with varying data set sizes?

- Section V-E: so, in this case CAMP leads to a worse first-attempt
 coverage. In Section V-C you did, however, show that CAMP leads to
 significantly better first-attempt coverage and also higher reserved
 capacity. What is an explanation for that fact that in this experiment
 we see tighter and smaller memory allocations for a number of tasks? This
 result comes across as being a bit contradictory.

- Section VIII: "to safely reduce cluster overprovisioning at scale". Given
 the focus of the policy and paper on avoiding underallocation and the
 fact that one of the results of Section V-C is that CAMP needs larger
 memory reservations compared to Sizey, I am not sure whether I agree with
 this overall statement.


