*8: Detailed comments for the authors
  This paper presents CAMP, a consumption-aware memory prediction framework for scientific workflows. CAMP uses eBPF kernel probes to capture fine-grained data consumption—explicit read bytes and memory-mapped page faults—and feeds this into a closed-loop memory prediction pipeline. It introduces hierarchical compatibility scopes, multi-quantile prediction, and a selective auditing policy to bound tracing overhead. Evaluation on thousands of bioinformatics workflow records under Slurm shows reduced OOM events, lower prediction error, and a live Bowtie2 cold-to-warm case study with large memory reduction.

Strengths:
1) Interesting problem. Memory over- and under-provisioning in HPC workflows causes OOM failures, wasted resources, and scheduling inefficiency. Better prediction is highly valuable.
2) Novel use of eBPF consumption data. Capturing actual read bytes and mmap-backed page faults provides a stronger signal than static file sizes or process names.
3) The multi-gate active learning policy is an interesting way to reduce tracing overhead while still collecting informative measurements.
4) The paper uses over 33,000 task records, multiple bioinformatics workflows, and both offline replay and a live Bowtie2 cold-to-warm experiment.
5) CAMP reduces median absolute percentage error by up to 40.31%, OOM events by 64.37%, and runtime-weighted memory wastage by 37.18%. The live experiment reduces requested memory by 71.96% while retaining 99.78% first-attempt coverage.

Weaknesses and questions:
1) CAMP underallocates fewer tasks at 50/50 and 85/15 but more at 30/70, 70/30, and 90/10. The 85/15 split also has high seed variation. This suggests the policy is not uniformly robust.
2) Compared with Sizey, CAMP’s request-to-peak ratio is 225.5% and unused memory time is 141.4%. This is a significant trade-off that must be considered.
3) Evaluation focuses on bioinformatics workflows. It is unclear how well CAMP generalizes to other scientific domains, HPC workloads, or non-Nextflow pipelines.
4) The first run uses a conservative default. The paper shows warm-run improvement but does not deeply analyze cold-start cost across diverse workflows.